apitap 0.57.0 (PyPI) vs walshadow 0.1.2 — capped PostgreSQL -> ClickHouse, CDC
raw log, generated 2026-10-04T03:33:56Z on the bench VPS
host: 16 cores, 61 GB RAM, shared with production

===== 1. the tools and their provenance
$ ~/apitap-057-pullback/bin/python -c 'import apitap; ...'
  apitap.__version__       0.57.0
  interpreter              3.13.3 (abi3 wheel)
  _apitap.abi3.so md5      41e9f7c252d3e1eb5403b70f87bf5435
  site-packages is bind-mounted READ-ONLY into the cage at /py and put on
  PYTHONPATH, so the PyPI wheel is what runs and nothing is built on this box.
  every leg re-asserts it from INSIDE the cage; e.g. the catch-up legs' own log:
    APITAP_VERSION 0.57.0
    APITAP_SO_MD5 41e9f7c252d3e1eb5403b70f87bf5435
    APITAP_TABLES 30

$ walshadow-stream --version   (from the release artifact, inside the image)
  walshadow-stream 0.1.2 (73329e4)
  release artifacts, sha256-verified against the published SHA256SUMS:
    walshadow-0.1.2-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu.tar.gz            (the daemon)
    walshadow-pgext-0.1.2-pg16-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu.tar.gz (the PG module)
  the pg16 module, because this campaign's source is PostgreSQL 16 and the
  shadow walshadow supervises is a physical copy of it — 'physical bootstrap
  cannot cross major versions' applies to the module as much as to the data.
  the image is the Debian-flavoured postgres:16, not the alpine one, because the
  published module links against glibc (ldd: libc.so.6) and cannot load into musl.
    f0645c15b80b20d55643bfd4dd285176488a3ca896cde910aa9747bcb61c7e01  walshadow-0.1.2-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu.tar.gz
    a1d42b9ad4979eb5cc753c4b982d6b5b50275a496c177e4c7e56c4f3a9d4cb22  walshadow-pgext-0.1.2-pg16-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu.tar.gz
    pg PostgreSQL 16.15 (Debian 16.15-1.pgdg13+2)

